# PHASE 5 — WhatsApp Cloud API

**Do not start this phase until I tell you Meta business verification is approved.**

### 5.1 Cloud API provider

`lib/messaging/providers/cloud-api.ts` sending approved templates:
`order_confirmation` (image header, Confirm / Request changes buttons),
`order_bill` (PDF document attachment), `due_reminder`, `ready_for_pickup`.
Config from env: `WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID`, `WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN`,
`WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID`, `WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN`,
`WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET`. Retry 5xx with backoff, log every send with its message id,
store outbound message ids on the order for webhook correlation.

It must satisfy the identical `MessagingProvider` interface with **zero changes to
any calling code**. If you find yourself needing to change a caller, stop and tell
me — that means the Phase 3 abstraction was wrong.

**Checkpoint — stop and report.**

### 5.2 Webhook

`/api/webhooks/whatsapp`. GET handles Meta's `hub.challenge`. POST verifies the
`X-Hub-Signature-256` HMAC against `WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET` and rejects failures.
This endpoint is public — treat every payload as hostile and validate with zod
before use. Button replies map back to an order via the tracked message id;
"Confirm" calls the **same** confirmation function the public page uses, not a
duplicate. Store delivery and read receipts. Always return 200 quickly, do slow
work after responding, and make handling idempotent because Meta retries.

**Checkpoint — stop and report.**

### 5.3 Switch on customer messaging

Reminder cron sends via the messaging provider, with per-notification-type toggles
in settings so the owner can turn customer messages on or off without a deploy.
Add a messaging log under settings showing every message sent: customer, type,
timestamp, status, cost category.

**Phase 5 acceptance:** a customer taps Confirm in WhatsApp and the order status
changes by itself, with no staff action.

